' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Same unit as a UML-RT capsule (typed ports + protocol) vs a UML component
'                 (provided/required interfaces).
'   usecase:      Teach protocol-governed (UML-RT) vs interface-based (UML component) design.
'   complexity:   variant
'   domain:       rose-realtime / uml-rt
' ============================================================================
title Example Rose RealTime - Capsule vs Component

note as TopNote
  VARIANT: same "Scheduler" unit shown two ways.
  LEFT: UML-RT CAPSULE - boundary ports typed by PROTOCOLS carrying SIGNALS.
       Protocol-governed, concurrent, with an executable state machine.
       Signals are asynchronous by default; timing/reactivity is first-class.
  RIGHT: UML COMPONENT - provided/required INTERFACES (method signatures).
       Interface-based, typically synchronous method calls; no built-in timing.
  Capsules suit real-time/embedded; components suit enterprise/OOP wiring.
end note

' ============ LEFT: UML-RT CAPSULE ============
class "Scheduler\n<<capsule>>" as RT_CAP {
  behavior: state machine
}
class "JobProtocol\n<<protocol>>" as RT_P {
  + in: submit(job), cancel(id)
  + out: completed(result)
}
class "TimerProtocol\n<<protocol>>" as RT_PT {
  + in: arm(ms)
  + out: timeout()
}
rectangle "p_job: JobProtocol" as RT_PORT1
rectangle "p_timer: TimerProtocol" as RT_PORT2

RT_CAP --> RT_PORT1 : "port"
RT_CAP --> RT_PORT2 : "port"
RT_PORT1 --> RT_P : "typed by"
RT_PORT2 --> RT_PT : "typed by"

' ============ RIGHT: UML COMPONENT ============
component "Scheduler\n<<component>>" as UML_COMP
interface "IJobService\n+ submit(job)\n+ cancel(id)" as IJOB
interface "ICompletionSink\n+ onCompleted(result)" as ISINK
interface "ITimer\n+ arm(ms)" as ITIMER

UML_COMP --() IJOB : "provides"
UML_COMP ..() ISINK : "requires"
UML_COMP ..() ITIMER : "requires"
